  <p>Creatine is one of the most studied supplements, and Jordan Hoppel recommends it for people who want to take it. This page is his coaching view, not medical advice. Get health advice from your doctor, someone measuring the levels in your body.</p>

  <h2>Dose</h2>
  <p>Start around 5 g a day and bump up as necessary. Alternatively, do a loading phase of 10 to 20 g for the first 10 days to saturate the body, then a low maintenance dose. Anecdotal and clinical research suggests up to 20 g a day helps people recoup after a poor night of sleep (brain fog, overall energy), and Jordan notes many recent studies related to Alzheimer's have been positive.</p>

  <h2>How and when to take it</h2>
  <p>Take it at a time you'll remember daily; consistency matters. Probably don't take it with certain minerals like calcium, since it may compete for absorption and be less efficient. Jordan typically tells people to take it in the morning because it's easier to remember, or after a workout, or at night.</p>

  <Media type="image" alt="Scoop of creatine monohydrate next to a glass of water" caption="Pick a time you'll remember." brief="Photo: creatine scoop and glass of water." />

  <h2>Weight gain and bloating</h2>
  <p>Most client questions are about bloating and weight gain. Creatine can make you put on weight because the body holds more water within the muscle, but it's not fat gain. Any bloating feeling would probably be due to something else, not the creatine.</p>

  <h2>Hair loss</h2>
  <p>Jordan says there's no real research behind it; it's user bias. People who work out tend to have higher testosterone, which is generally accompanied by hair loss, and the chance they also take creatine is high, so they're associative factors rather than causative.</p>

  <h2>Creatine for women</h2>
  <p>The only distinction is that women might not need as much, since women generally tend to be smaller overall than men.</p>

  <JordanQuote>Creatine is very highly studied. People who want to take it should.</JordanQuote>
